### 2\. Theory

Git is a distributed version control system (VCS) used to track changes in source code, maintain different versions of a project, and support collaboration among developers. Git allows developers to work on separate branches and merge their changes when the work is completed.

GitHub is an online platform used to host Git repositories and collaborate with other developers.

Git maintains three important areas:

* Working Directory: The folder where project files are created or modified.

* Staging Area: The area where changes are selected before committing.

* Local Repository: The area where committed changes are stored in Git history.

Working Directory

Create or modify files

`git add`

Staging Area

Select changes for the next commit

`git commit`

Local Repository

Save a version in Git history

`git push` / `git pull`

Remote Repository (GitHub)

Share and synchronize code

### 3\. Git Commands and Their Uses

| Command    | Purpose                                                   |
| ---------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |
| git init   | Initializes a new local Git repository.                   |
| git add    | Adds changes to the staging area.                         |
| git commit | Saves staged changes in local repository history.         |
| git push   | Uploads local commits to a remote repository.             |
| git pull   | Fetches and integrates changes from a remote repository.  |
| git branch | Creates or lists branches.                                |
| git merge  | Combines changes from one branch into another.            |
| git diff   | Displays differences between file versions or Git states. |
| git status | Shows the current state of files and staging.             |
| git log    | Displays commit history.                                  |

