Aim: To install and configure Jenkins, create a pipeline using a `Jenkinsfile` stored in a remote GitHub repository, and deploy a web application using Docker as one of the pipeline stages.

Theory: Jenkins is an automation server used for Continuous Integration and Continuous Delivery (CI/CD). A `Jenkinsfile` defines the pipeline stages. GitHub stores the source code and Jenkinsfile, while Docker packages the web application into an image and runs it inside a container. Jenkins can periodically check the GitHub repository for changes using Poll SCM.

### 1\. Requirements

* Windows PC with administrator permission, if installation requires it.

* Java version supported by the selected Jenkins release.

* Jenkins, Git, Docker Desktop and an internet connection.

* A GitHub repository containing the web application and `Jenkinsfile`.

### 2\. Install and verify the software

Install Jenkins from the official [Jenkins website](https://www.jenkins.io/download/?utm_source=chatgpt.com) and Docker Desktop from [Docker's website](https://www.docker.com/products/docker-desktop/?utm_source=chatgpt.com). Install Git from [git-scm.com](https://git-scm.com/install/windows?utm_source=chatgpt.com).

Open PowerShell and check:

```powershell
java -version
git --version
docker --version
docker info
```

Start Docker Desktop and wait until its engine is running. If `docker info` fails, resolve that issue before running the pipeline.

Start Jenkins if it is installed as a Windows service:

```powershell
Get-Service Jenkins
```

If it is stopped, open PowerShell as administrator and run:

```powershell
Start-Service Jenkins
```

Open Jenkins at `http://localhost:8080` if that is the configured port. Complete the initial setup and log in.

### 3\. Create the GitHub repository

Create a GitHub repository named `jenkins-docker-practical`. Add these three files to its root directory.

File 1: `index.html`

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>ShopEasy</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Welcome to ShopEasy</h1>
    <p>Deployed using Jenkins and Docker.</p>
</body>
</html>
```

File 2: `Dockerfile`

```dockerfile
FROM nginx:alpine
COPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html
EXPOSE 80
```

File 3: `Jenkinsfile`

```groovy
pipeline {
    agent any

    triggers {
        pollSCM('H/5 * * * *')
    }

    stages {
        stage('Checkout') {
            steps {
                checkout scm
            }
        }

        stage('Build Docker Image') {
            steps {
                bat 'docker build -t shopeasy:latest .'
            }
        }

        stage('Deploy Web Application') {
            steps {
                bat '''
                    docker rm -f shopeasy-container 2>NUL
                    docker run -d --name shopeasy-container -p 8081:80 shopeasy:latest
                '''
            }
        }

        stage('Verify Deployment') {
            steps {
                bat 'docker ps'
                bat 'curl.exe -f http://localhost:8081'
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Commit and push all three files to the repository's `main` branch.

The pipeline uses port 8081 because port 8080 is commonly used by Jenkins itself. If port 8081 is already occupied, select another available host port and change the URL in the verification stage accordingly.

### 4\. Create the Jenkins pipeline job

* Open Jenkins.

* Select New Item.

* Enter the job name `github-docker-pipeline`.

* Select Pipeline and click OK.

* Scroll to the Build Triggers section and enable Poll SCM.

* Enter this schedule:

```text
H/5 * * * *
```

This checks the repository approximately every five minutes. Jenkins builds when it detects relevant changes; it does not necessarily run a build every five minutes if nothing changes.

### 5\. Configure the remote Jenkinsfile

Under the job's Pipeline section, select:

* Definition: Pipeline script from SCM

* SCM: Git

* Repository URL: `https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/jenkins-docker-practical.git`

* Credentials: None for a public repository; use valid GitHub credentials for a private repository.

* Branch Specifier: `*/main`

* Script Path: `Jenkinsfile`

Replace `YOUR-USERNAME` with your actual GitHub username. Click Save.

The Jenkinsfile must be present at the specified path in the selected branch.

### 6\. Run the pipeline

* Open `github-docker-pipeline`.

* Click Build Now.

* Open the latest build and select Console Output.

* Confirm that the stages complete successfully:

    * Checkout

    * Build Docker Image

    * Deploy Web Application

    * Verify Deployment

If Jenkins reports a Docker connection error, check that Docker Desktop is running and that the Windows account running the Jenkins service has permission to access Docker. Merely running Docker successfully in your own PowerShell session does not guarantee that the Jenkins service can access it.

### 7\. Verify the deployed application

Open PowerShell and run:

```powershell
docker images
docker ps
curl.exe http://localhost:8081
```

You should see the `shopeasy` image, a running container named `shopeasy-container`, and the HTML response from the web application.

Open the browser:

[http://localhost:8081](http://localhost:8081?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

You should see Welcome to ShopEasy.

### 8\. Test automatic polling

* Open `index.html` on GitHub.

* Change the heading and commit the change to `main`.

* Wait for Jenkins to detect the change.

* Open Build History → Console Output and confirm that the pipeline runs again.

* Refresh the application at `http://localhost:8081` to verify the updated content.

A manual Build Now confirms that the pipeline can run. A new build triggered after a GitHub commit confirms that polling works.

### Result

A Jenkins pipeline was configured to retrieve a Jenkinsfile from a remote GitHub repository, build a Docker image, deploy the web application in a Docker container, and verify the deployment. Poll SCM was configured to check for repository changes approximately every five minutes.

### Viva questions

1\. What is Jenkins? Jenkins is an automation server used to implement CI/CD pipelines.

2\. What is a Jenkinsfile? A Jenkinsfile is a text file that defines pipeline stages and steps, usually written in Declarative or Scripted Pipeline syntax.

3\. What is Pipeline script from SCM? It allows Jenkins to retrieve the Jenkinsfile from a source control repository such as GitHub.

4\. What is Poll SCM? Poll SCM periodically checks the repository for changes and triggers a build when changes are detected.

5\. What is Docker? Docker is a platform for packaging and running applications in containers.

6\. What is the difference between an image and a container? An image is the packaged application template; a container is a running instance of that image.

7\. Why is port mapping used? The mapping `-p 8081:80` forwards requests from host port 8081 to port 80 inside the container.

8\. What does `docker build` do? It builds a Docker image using the instructions in the Dockerfile.

9\. What does `docker run` do? It creates and starts a container from an image.

10\. Why use a remote Jenkinsfile? It keeps pipeline instructions in version control, allowing them to be reviewed, tracked, and updated alongside the application.

Important: This pipeline replaces the existing container named `shopeasy-container` whenever deployment runs. Use that name only for this practical, not for an unrelated container. On a college PC, confirm Docker Desktop and the Jenkins service can both run before beginning the assessment.
